2. System Algorithms
Here is the step-by-step logic (Algorithms) for the core functionalities of the application.
Algorithm A: Google OAuth & Role-Based Routing
Goal: Authenticate the user securely and show them the correct dashboard based on their email.
 * Start: User clicks "Sign in with Google".
 * Action: Frontend redirects to Google's OAuth 2.0 server.
 * Response: Google authenticates the user and returns a secure JWT (JSON Web Token) to the frontend.
 * Action: Frontend sends this JWT to your Backend API (e.g., Java Spring Boot).
 * Validation: Backend verifies the token signature with Google.
 * Database Lookup:
   * Backend extracts the email from the token.
   * SELECT role FROM users WHERE email = extracted_email
   * If email exists in students table -> Role is STUDENT.
   * Else If email exists in d_group_staff table -> Role is FACILITATOR.
   * Else -> Return Error ("User not found in system").
 * Response: Backend generates a session token and sends the Role back to the frontend.
 * End: Frontend hides the login screen and unhides the specific dashboard (Student or Facilitator) based on the received Role.
Algorithm B: Update Task Status (D-Group Facilitator)
Goal: Allow staff to mark a pending maintenance task as completed.
 * Start: Facilitator clicks the "Mark as Done" button next to a task on their dashboard.
 * Action: Frontend extracts the task_id from the clicked item.
 * API Call: Frontend sends a PUT /api/tasks/{task_id} request to the backend with data {"status": "COMPLETED"}.
 * Database Execution:
   * Backend runs: UPDATE facility_tasks SET status = 'COMPLETED' WHERE task_id = requested_task_id;
 * Response: Backend returns a success message (HTTP 200 OK).
 * UI Update: Frontend changes the UI badge from "Pending" (Yellow) to "Completed" (Green) without refreshing the page.
 * End.
3. Application Use Cases
A "Use Case" defines how different actors (users) interact with the system to achieve a specific goal.
Actor 1: College Student
 * Use Case 1: View Academic Profile
   * Description: Student logs in to see their real-time CGPA, total credits earned, and department details pulled from the students and enrollments tables.
 * Use Case 2: Check Upcoming Exams & Courses
   * Description: Student checks the dashboard to see which courses they are enrolled in for the current semester (from the courses table).
 * Use Case 3: View Hostel Allocation
   * Description: Student checks which hostel block they are assigned to, the warden's contact info, and pending hostel fees.
Actor 2: D-Group Facilitator (Maintenance / Cleaning Staff)
 * Use Case 4: View Daily Work Roster
   * Description: Staff logs in and views a list of pending tasks assigned specifically to their staff_id for their current shift.
 * Use Case 5: Update Task Status
   * Description: Staff completes sweeping a lab or fixing a pipe and clicks "Completed" on their phone to update the system.
 * Use Case 6: Report Infrastructure Damage
   * Description: While cleaning, staff notices a broken window. They use the portal to create a new task ticket categorized as "Damage" for the admin to review.
Actor 3: College Administrator (Implicit Backend User)
 * Use Case 7: Bulk Assign Tasks
   * Description: Admin assigns weekly cleaning schedules to various D-group staff members across different department buildings.
 * Use Case 8: Monitor Campus Operations
   * Description: Admin views a high-level dashboard showing how many facility tasks are pending vs. completed today to measure staff efficiency.
